lyrics:
Bless-ed is the one who does not walk within the wicked way,
Nor stand in the path of sinners, nor sit where the scoffers stay.
But he takes delight in foll’wing what the Lord’s law says is right,
On that law he meditates both in the day and in the night.

He will be just like a tree that’s planted by the streams that flow;
Yielding fruit within its season, leaves that ever healthy grow.
In all things he does, he prospers; but the wicked are not so;
They are like the useless chaff that’s cast away by winds that blow.

Thus, the wicked will not stand when comes the final Judgment Day;
Nor will sinners join the gath’ring of the righteous in the Way.
For the Lord knows well and watches o’er the way the righteous own;
But the way the wicked choose will perish and be overthrown.

Lord, our Lord, majestic is Your name throughout the whole wide earth;
You display and set Your splendor o’er the heav’ns which show Your worth.
From the mouths of infant children, You establish strength and praise;
Make the enemy and vengeful cease before Your wondrous ways.

When I see Your glor-ious heavens, moon and stars which You ordain;
What is man that You regard him, son of man with care maintain?
Yet You made him slightly lower than the angels high above;
Crowning him with glory, honor, just beneath the God of love.

You made him to rule creation, put all things beneath his feet—
Sheep and oxen, beasts and cattle, birds of heav’n and fish of sea;
All that swim within the pathways of the sea declare Your worth.
Lord, our Lord, majestic is Your name throughout the whole wide earth.

I’ll praise You, Lord, give thanks with all my heart;
Tell of Your wonders, and exult in Thee.
I will be glad, praise to Your name impart;
O, Lord Most High, I sing and worship Thee.

My foes turn back, they stumble before Thee;
For You do see and maintain my just cause.
You sit on high, and judging righteously,
You rebuke nations who do spurn Your laws.

You have destroyed the wicked enemy;
You blotted out their name forevermore,
Uprooted all their cities thoroughly.
Ruin has come—their mem’ry is no more.

But the Lord reigns enthroned in justice pure;
He judges all the world in righteousness.
His upright judgment is established sure;
He judges people with true uprightness.

The Lord’s a refuge for oppressed ones, too;
He is their stronghold in their time of need.
Let those who know Your name put trust in You;
You don’t forsake those who seek You indeed.

Sing to the Lord, enthroned in Zion’s place;
Declare among the peoples all His deeds.
He who avenges blood still sees their face;
Cries of afflicted ones the Lord God heeds.

Lord, see my enemies, and don’t forget;
See my affliction from the ones who hate.
Have mercy—lift me from the gates of death,
That I may tell Your praise in Zion’s gate.

I’ll find joy in Your saving work alone;
The nations sink into the pit they’ve made.
The Lord has made Himself be clearly known;
Their foot is caught within the net they laid.

The Lord is known by justice that stands firm;
In their own work the wicked are ensnared.Selah
All who forget God to She-ol return;
But poor and needy will not perish there.

Arise, O Lord, don’t let mere man prevail.
Let nations come, be judged before Your throne.
Strike them with fear, Lord—let the nations know
they are but men, and You are God alone. Selah

Help, Lord, for godly ones are gone, the faithful are no more;
And everyone speaks flatt’ring lies, which from their lips do pour.

May God the Lord end flatt’ring lips and tongues that boast great things;
That say, “Our tongues prevail, so who can lordship o’er us bring?”

“Because the needy are oppressed,” the Lord says, “I’ll arise!
I will protect and keep them safe from all those who malign.”

The Lord’s words are both true and pure, like silver that’s refined;
They’re pure like silver fired within a furnace seven times.

O Lord, You will protect and save from wicked far and near,
For sinners freely strut about when vileness is revered.

O keep me safe, my God—I take refuge in Thee.
I said to God, “You are my Lord; I have no good but Thee.”
As for Your saints on earth, they are the glo-rious ones;
The godly who are my delight—Your daughters and Your sons.

Those who seek other gods, their sorrows will increase,
For chasing after emptiness will never bring them peace.
I will not join with them, blood offerings to make;
The names of idols, empty gods, on my lips I’ll not take.

The Lord‘s my portion and my cup, my sure defense;
For You have made my lot secure – You’re my inheritance.
The bound’ries of my life have fallen pleasantly;
My heritage, my sure repose, is beautiful to me.

I’ll praise and bless the Lord who has instructed me;
Indeed, my heart and mind all night do counsel give to me.
For I have set the Lord before me e’er to stand;
And I will never shaken be, for He’s at my right hand.

Therefore, my heart is glad, my spirit does rejoice;
My flesh will also dwell secure, and I’ll lift up my voice.
You won’t abandon me to death and the grave’s sway;
You won’t allow Your Holy One to undergo decay.

You have made known to me the path of life, the Way;
For in Your presence there is joy in all its full array.
At Your right hand are found both joy and pleasures’ store;
For in Your presence, at Your hand, are pleasures evermore.

My God, my God, O why have You forsaken me, and why
are You so far from saving me, while groaning words I cry?

All day, my God, to You I cry, yet I’m not heard by Thee;
And in the darkness of the night, I cannot silent be.

But You are holy, and You are enthroned in Isr’el’s praise.
Our fathers put their trust in You—deliv’rance You did raise.

When unto You they sent their cry, to them deliv’rance came;
Because they put their trust in You, they were not put to shame.

But as for me, I am a worm; a man not recognized;
I am reproached by men, and by the people am despised.

All those who see me laugh in scorn; hurl insults all the day;
They wag and shake their heads at me; and mocking me, they say:

“His trust is in the Lord, that He would free him by His might;
Let Him deliver him, if He in him does take delight.”

But You brought me out of the womb, and made me trust in Thee;
And even at my mother’s breast You did take care of me.

From birth I was upon You cast, ev’n from the womb till now;
From mother’s womb You’ve been my God; and still my God art Thou.

Be not far off, for grief is near, and none to help is found.
Bulls compass me; bulls of Bashan encircle me around.

The lions’ mouths are opened wide against me all the day,
Like as a lion ravaging and roaring for his prey.

Like water I’m poured out, my bones all out of joint do part;
Like wax within my inward parts, so melted is my heart.

My strength is like a potsherd dried—my tongue impedes my breath,
It sticks to my mouth’s roof—You lay me in the dust of death.

For dogs have compassed me about, and wicked men did meet;
A band of men encircled me; they pierced my hands and feet.

I can count all my bones; and those who gloat upon me stare;
They cast lots for my clothing and divide my garments there.

Be not far off, O Lord, my strength; come quickly and help me;
Deliver me from sword and dogs, my precious life set free.

Come rescue me from lions—from the mouth of lions strong,
And from the horns of oxen wild that threaten all day long.

I will declare Your name unto my brethren everywhere;
Amidst the congregation round, Your praise I will declare.

All you who fear the Lord, praise Him; revere and glory tell;
All Jacob’s children, honor Him—offspring of Is-ra-el.

For He did not despise nor scorn th’afflicted’s suffering;
Nor hid His face from him, but heard the cry which he did bring.

Within the congregation great, my praise shall be of Thee;
Before all those who fear You, I’ll fulfill my vows to Thee.

The poor shall eat, and shall be filled; those seeking God will give
praise to the Lord; all you who seek—may your hearts ever live!

And all the ends of earth shall turn, remembering the Lord;
All fam’lies of the nations will bow down and praise accord.

For all dominion far and near belongs to Him alone.
The Lord rules over nations all, from His almighty throne.

The rich of earth will worship Him, all who to dust descend;
And all shall kneel—none of them can their soul from death defend.

Descendants, then, will all serve Him—the children yet to be;
They will be told about the Lord, future posterity.

For they shall come, and shall declare His truth and righteousness
unto a people yet unborn, for He has done all this.

The Lord’s my Shepherd; I have all I need;
He makes me lie down in the pastures green.
Beside still waters, He does gently lead;
Restores my soul when I upon Him lean.

He guides me in the paths of righteousness;
For His name’s sake, gives guidance unto me.
Yea, though I walk through paths of darkest death,
I fear no evil, for You are with me.

Your rod and staff give comfort unto me.
You serve a feast in presence of my foes;
My head anoint with oil for all to see;
My heart is full, and my cup overflows.

Surely Your goodness, and Your mercy, Lord,
Will follow me for all my days and more;
And I will dwell in the house of the Lord,
Dwell in His house and presence evermore.

The Lord’s my shepherd, I’ll not want; He makes me down to lie
in pastures green; He leadeth me the quiet waters by.

My soul He doth restore again; and me to walk doth make
within the paths of righteousness, ev’n for His own name’s sake.

Yea, though I walk in death’s dark vale, yet will I fear no ill;
For Thou art with me; and Thy rod and staff me comfort still.

My table Thou has furnish-ed in presence of my foes;
My head Thou dost with oil anoint, and my cup overflows.

Goodness and mercy all my life shall surely follow me;
And in God’s house forevermore my dwelling place shall be.

Ascribe to the Lord, all you ones of great might;
Ascribe to the Lord glory, strength in your sight.
Ascribe to Him glory, and His name adore;
In splendor of holiness, worship the Lord.

The voice of the Lord is above the great sea;
He thunders in glory; o’er waters is He!
The voice of the Lord, full of power and might;
His voice is majestic and flaming in light.

The voice of the Lord breaks the cedars in two;
The cedars of Lebanon, He does subdue.
The Lord makes all Lebanon skip like a deer.
Mount Hermon is like a young ox when He’s near.

The voice of the Lord strikes with flashing of light.
His voice shakes the desert of Kadesh with might.
The voice of the Lord strips the forests all bare;
And all in His temple, His glory declare.

The Lord sits enthroned o’er the flood – He is King.
The Lord on His throne sits forever as King.
The Lord gives His people His strength without cease.
The Lord is the One who will bless them with peace.

In You, O Lord, I take refuge, let me be never put to shame;
And in Your righteousness, O Lord, deliver me—I trust Your name.
O come and turn Your ear to me; come quickly and my rescue be;
And be my Rock of refuge sure, a fortress strong to rescue me.

Since You’re my fortress and my Rock, for Your name’s sake, lead me and guide;
Free me from traps they set for me, for You’re the strength where I abide.
For I commit my spirit, Lord, into Your sovereign, steadfast hands;
Redeem me, Lord, O God of truth; my life before You ever stands.

I hate those who to idols cling; I trust in God the Lord alone;
I will be glad, rejoice and sing in Your love, for I am Your own.
For You saw my affliction and You knew the anguish of my soul;
You did not hand me to my foe, but set my feet in places full.

Be merciful to me, O Lord, for I am in distress and grief.
My soul and body fill with grief; my eyes with sorrow do grow weak.
My life’s consumed by anguish and my years by groans as You I seek.
My strength fails from affliction and my very bones grow sick and weak.

Because of all my enemies, my neighbors pour contempt on me;
I am a dread e’en to my friends—when they see me, they turn and flee.
I am forgotten as though dead; I’m like old broken pottery.
I hear their slander; I’m in dread; for they conspire and plot ’gainst me.

But I will trust in You, O Lord; I say, “You are my God indeed.”
My times are in Your hands for sure; deliver me from enemies.
Let Your face shine upon Your child; in Your unfailing love, save me;
And let me not be put to shame, for I have cried out, Lord, to Thee.

Let shame upon the wicked come; let them lie silent in the grave;
Let lying lips be silenced, for with pride and arrogance they rave.
How great Your goodness which You store for those who fear and reverence You;
Which You bestow in sight of all, on those who take refuge in You.

For in the shelter of Your face, You hide them from intrigues of men;
You keep them in Your dwelling place; safe from accusing tongues of men.
Praise to the Lord, for He has shown His love when I was under siege.
In my alarm, I cried alone, “I’m cut off and You don’t see me!”

Yet, You did surely hear my cry for mercy; when I call, You hear.
O love the Lord, all you His saints! The Lord saves faithful who draw near.
But all the proud He pays in full; He pays them back their due reward.
Be strong, O faithful, and take heart; all you who put hope in the Lord.

Bless-ed is the one whose sin is covered over by the Lord,
Whose transgressions are forgiven, and whose guilt God won’t record.
Bless-ed is the one whose sin the Lord does not count against him,
In whose spirit no deceit or hidden sin is found within.

When I held my sin inside me, and kept silent in my way,
Then my bones grew weak and wasted through my groaning all the day.
For Your hand was heavy on me; day and night, my strength did lapse;
Sapped away as in the heat of summer’s hot, lethargic grasp.Selah

Then I told You of my sin, and did not hide iniquity;
I said, “I’ll confess my trespass”—You forgave, purged guilt from me.Selah
Therefore, let the godly pray to You while You may still be found;
Surely waters will not reach them, mighty waves that surge around.

For You are my place of hiding, You protect me from all strife;
And with songs of Your deliv’rance, You surround and keep my life.Selah
“I will teach and will instruct you in the way that you should go;
I will counsel and watch o’er you; the right path to you I’ll show.

“Don’t be like the horse or mule that do not understand at all;
Lest they’re led by bit and bridle, they won’t come when you do call.”
Many woes afflict the wicked, but the Lord’s unfailing love
does surround the ones who trust Him. Sing! Rejoice in God above!

An oracle speaks deep within the heart of wicked man;
No fear of God before his eyes—in sinfulness he stands.

In his own eyes, he does not see or comprehend his sin;
He flatters self until he thinks that he is good within.

The words he speaks are wicked and deceit within them lies;
For he no longer does what’s good, and is no longer wise.

While on his bed, he plots to do much evil all along;
Commits himself to sinful things; does not reject what’s wrong.

Your love, O Lord, does reach the heav’ns; Your faithfulness the skies;
Your righteousness like mountains great; Your justice deep does lie.

Lord, You preserve both man and beast—Your love’s beyond all things!
Both high and low find refuge in the shadow of Your wings.

They feast with great abundance—from Your house, they’re satisfied;
And from Your river of delights, You for them drink provide.

For with You is the fount of life, in Your light, we see light.
Continue Your unfailing love to those who walk upright.

Don’t let the foot of proud ones come against me every day;
Nor let the hand of wicked ones drive me with them away.

See how the evildoers all are fallen where they lie.
They are thrown down, and never will be able to arise.

Lord, don’t rebuke me in Your wrath, in anger don’t chide me;
Your arrows pierce me and Your hand is heavy upon me.

Because of Your great wrath, I’m sick; my bones are weak from sin;
My guilt o’erwhelms me like a weight, too deep to bear within.

My wounds, they fester loathsomely, because of sinful pride.
I am bowed down, brought very low; and mourn all day inside.

My back is filled with searing pain; in me no health is found;
I’m feeble and crushed utterly; my anguished groans abound.

My longings lie before You, Lord, and You hear all my sighs;
With pounding heart, my strength fails me; the light’s gone from my eyes.

My friends and neighbors stay away, because they see my lot.
Foes set their traps and seek my life, and all day long they plot.

I’m like the deaf who cannot hear; like mute, who cannot speak;
I’m like the one who does not hear, whose mouth no words can speak.

I wait for You, O Lord, my God, and You will answer me;
For I said, “Do not let them gloat; exalt themselves o’er me.”

For I am just about to fall; my pain’s always within;
So I confess iniquity; I’m troubled by my sin.

Too many are my enemies, who hate me without cause;
They pay me evil for my good; and slander without pause.

O Lord, do not forsake me now, and be not far from me.
O Lord, my Savior, hear me now; come quickly to help me!

I said, “I will watch all my ways, and keep my tongue from sin;
I’ll put a muzzle on my mouth while with such wicked men.”

But while I silent was and still, not even speaking good,
The anguish in my soul increased, though try as best I could.

My heart grew hotter within me, as, thinking one by one
of all my troubles, the fire burned; then I spoke with my tongue:

“Show me, O Lord, my life’s short end, the number of my days;
Make me to know how fast it flies—how fleeting are my days.

“For You have made life but a breath; my days are a short span;
My years are nothing before You, a mere breath is each man.Selah

“So, phantomlike we go about, we scurry all in vain;
We heap up wealth, but don’t know who will end up with our gain.

“But now, Lord, what do I look for? My only hope’s in You;
Save me from my transgressions, and the scorn of fools subdue.

“I’m silent, and keep my mouth closed, for all this You have done.
Remove Your scourge from me, for by Your hand I’m overcome.

“For You rebuke and discipline all men for all their sin;
And You consume our wealth like moths—we are but breath and wind.Selah

“O hear my prayer and listen, Lord, to You for help I cry;
Don’t be deaf to my tears, for like a foreigner am I.

“I’m a sojourner with You as my fathers were before.
Turn Your gaze so I may rejoice before I am no more.”

I waited for the Lord my God, I waited patiently;
And He in mercy heard my cry; inclined His ear to me.
He brought me up out of the pit, out from the miry clay;
He set my feet upon a rock, there firm to stand and stay.

He put a new song in my mouth, God’s praise for all to hear;
And many then will trust the Lord who see and learn to fear.
How bless’d the one who trusts the Lord, who looks not to the proud;
Nor to the ones who turn aside to false gods all around.

For many are Your wonders, Lord, things planned beyond compare;
They are far more than I can count, more than I could declare.
An off’ring You have not required, but rather pierced my ears;
Burnt off’ring You have not desired, but rather, one who hears.

And so I said, “Behold, I come; it is prescribed for me
within Your scroll to do Your will; Your law is deep in me.”
I have proclaimed deliverance, glad news for all to hear;
You know I’ve not restrained my lips from speaking far and near.

I did not hide Your righteousness alone within my heart;
But I spoke of Your faithfulness; Your truth I did impart.
I’ve not concealed Your steadfast love, Your faithfulness of old;
To the assembly gathered round, I’ve Your salvation told.

You’ll not withhold Your mercy, Lord, or keep it far from me;
Your steadfast love and faithfulness keep me continually.
For troubles have surrounded me; iniquities flood me
more than the hairs upon my head; my heart fails within me.

Be pleased, O Lord, to save me and come quickly to my aid;
For many seek to take my life—let them now be dismayed.
Let those be turned back, put to shame, who in my harm delight;
Leave them appalled and desolate who would destroy my life.

Let those who say to me, “Aha!” be shocked at their own shame.
But may those seeking You rejoice, and be glad in Your name.
May those who love salvation say, “Exalt the Lord always”!
Yet I am needy, think of me! My God, do not delay!

Be merciful to me, O God, for vile men pursue me;
All day they come with their attack, oppress me violently.

They slander me as they pursue, all day they’ve cruelly lied;
For many are attacking me with arrogance and pride.

When I’m afraid, I’ll trust in You, in God whose word I praise.
In God I trust; I will not fear. What harm can mere men raise?

But all day long they twist my words, they plot to bring me strife.
They lurk and watch my steps, conspire to take away my life.

On no account let them escape, in anger bring them down;
Because of wickedness, O God, cast them out and confound.

For You see all my tossings and upon my plight You look;
Put my tears in Your bottle, God – Are they not in Your book?

Then all my foes will be turned back, when for Your help I call;
For this I know – that God’s for me, and will not let me fall.

In God, the Lord, whose word I praise, I’ll trust most faithfully.
In God I trust; I will not fear. What can man do to me?

I’m under vows to You, O God; I will present to You
the of-fer-ings that give You thanks—to my vows I’ll be true.

For You delivered me from death, my feet from stumbling strife;
That I may walk before You, God, within the light of life.

Be merciful to me, O God, be merciful to me;
My soul takes refuge, God, in You—the shadow of Your wings,
Until disaster all is past; destruction passes by.
To God who will fulfill His plan, I cry to God Most High.

For He will send from heav’n above salvation unto me;
He will rebuke all those who do attack and trample me. Selah
For God sends forth His steadfast love and faithfulness to me;
His lovingkindness and His truth – with me they’ll always be.

I lie among the rav’nous beasts, in midst of lions, Lord;
Amidst cruel men whose teeth are spears, whose tongues are like sharp swords.
Above the heavens, O my God, may You exalted be;
Your glory over all the earth—let all the peoples see.

They have prepared a net for me—for my steps to be caught;
My soul is bowed down low because a pit for me they sought.
They dug a pit before my path, but see what God has done!
They’ve fallen in the pit themselves, into it they have run.Selah

My heart is steadfast, O my God, and I will sing Your praise!
Awake, my soul, wake harp and lyre; I will awake the day!
And I will give thanks unto You, O Lord, midst people all,
I will sing praises unto You among the nations all.

Your lovingkindness does extend beyond the heav’ns above;
Your faithfulness unto the clouds that shower forth Your love.
Above the heavens, O my God, may You exalted be;
Your glory over all the earth—let all the peoples see!

You rulers, do you speak what’s just? Judge rightly among men?
No, in your heart’s unrighteousness, violence is in your hand.
The wicked go astray from birth; speak lies right from the womb;
Their words like venom of a snake that sends you to the tomb.

Their venom’s like a cobra who will stop its ear so well,
That it can’t hear the charmer’s voice, or caster of a spell.
O God, come shatter all their teeth, let their mouths speak no more;
Break out the fangs of lions, God, and shut their mouths, O Lord.

Let them be like the water that runs off and flows away;
When they shoot arrows, let them be made dull along the way.
Like slugs that melt, like stillborn birth that never sees the sun
Before pots feel the heat of thorns, they’re swept away and gone.

The righteous will rejoice when they God’s vengeance do observe;
They’ll wash their feet in blood of wicked ones who did truth spurn.
Then men will say, “Now we can see the fruit of righteousness;
There surely is a God who’ll judge the earth in faithfulness.”

Hear me, O God, as unto You I voice complaint and cry;
Protect my life from enemies whose threats are always nigh.
Hide me from their conspiracy – that noisy, evil crowd,
They sharpen tongues like swords and aim their words like arrows proud.

They shoot their arrows and ambush the innocent who’s near;
They shoot at him quite suddenly and show no dread or fear.
They talk to one another and encourage evil plans;
They say, “Who sees the snares we hide? We’ve formed a perfect plan!”

They plot injustice and declare, “Our perfect plot will stand!”
For surely very cunning is the mind and heart of man.
But God will shoot His arrows, too, and they will be struck down;
He’ll turn their own tongues back on them – their plans He will confound.

To ruin God will bring them – those who see will scorn each one;
Then all will fear and ponder God, proclaiming what He’s done.
Let righteous ones take refuge and rejoice in God the LORD;
Let all upright in heart praise Him – to Him all praise accord!

Sing for joy to God our strength, to God of Jacob shout aloud!
Strike the tambourine and play the harp and lyre—let praise abound!
Sound the ram’s horn at the New Moon, and the Feast when moon is full.
This is God’s decree for Isr’el—Jacob’s God did give this law.

He established it for Joseph, as a statute He decreed;
When He went forth against Egypt, where the voice was strange indeed.
He said, “I removed the burden; from hard tasks their hands set free;
In distress, you called—I answered, and I tested you when free.Selah

“Hear, O people, I will warn you—if you would but listen well;
You shall not have gods among you—no strange god, O Israel!
I’m the Lord your God who brought you up from Egypt by My hand;
Open wide your mouth—I’ll fill it, so that you can always stand.

“But My people would not listen; they would not submit to Me.
So I gave them over to their stubborn hearts, their own decree.
If My people would but listen, if they’d follow in My ways,
Then I would subdue their en’mies, turn their foes back in disgrace!

“Those who hate the Lord would cringe before Him whom they should adore;
And their punishment would last forever and forevermore.
But you would be fed with goodness, finest wheat I’d feed to you;
And sweet honey from the rock, I gladly would provide for you.”

God takes His stand and judges in the midst of “gods” justly:
“How long will you unjustly judge; show partiality?Selah

“Defend and vindicate the weak, those who are fatherless;
Maintain the rights of those in need, and those who are oppressed.

“Rescue the weak and needy ones, for they need help to stand;
Deliver them from the strong grip of evil, wicked hands.

“They do not know nor understand; in darkness walk about;
So the foundations of the earth are shaken all throughout.

“I said, ‘You’re gods’; you are all sons of God the Lord Most High.
Yet you’ll fall like all rulers fall; and like mere men, you’ll die.”

Rise up, O God, and judge the earth—You reign o’er nations all;
They are Your own inheritance, and You possess them all.

Hear me, O Lord, and answer me; I’m poor and in great need.
Come, guard my life; You are my God—save me! I trust in Thee.

Have mercy, Lord, I call to You all day with my heart full;
Restore my joy, for unto You, O Lord, I lift my soul.

For You are good and gracious, Lord, and ready to forgive;
And You abound in love to all who call on You to live.

O hear my prayer, Lord, as I cry for mercy unto Thee;
When trouble comes, I call to You, for You will answer me.

Among the gods there’s none like You; for only You are Lord;
And neither are there deeds that can compare at all with Yours.

All nations You have made will come and worship rev’rently,
They will bring glory to Your name; O Lord, they’ll worship Thee.

For You alone are great, O God, and marv’lous are Your deeds.
For You alone are God o’er all; Your glory all exceeds.

Teach me Your way, Lord, and I’ll walk in Your truth all my days;
Give me an undivided heart to fear Your name always.

O Lord my God, with all my heart, to You I will give praise;
And I will glorify Your name forever and always.

Great is the love You have toward me; Your love that acts to save.
For You have saved me from She-ol; from the depths of the grave.

The arrogant attack me, God—a band of ruthless ones;
They seek my life with no regard for You and all You’ve done.

But You, O Lord, are merciful and full of graciousness;
To anger, slow; abundant in both love and faithfulness.

O turn to me with mercy, for my hope on You is stayed;
Grant strength unto Your servant, save the son of Your handmaid.

Give me a sign of good from You, so that my foes will see
and be ashamed, O Lord, for You do help and comfort me.

I will sing Your love and justice; to You, Lord, I will sing praise;
I’ll be careful to be blameless. When will You, Lord, fill my days?
I will walk within my house with blameless heart, integrity;
Set no wrong before my eyes; no evil deeds will cling to me.

Deeds of faithless men I hate, their perverse hearts I do despise;
I have naught to do with evil; it shall be far from my eyes.
Those who secretly do slander, I will silence them for sure;
All those who have haughty eyes and proud hearts, I will not endure.

My eyes will be on the faithful—they will ever dwell with me.
And all those whose walk is blameless, they will minister to me.
No one who acts with deceit will dwell within my house or land;
No one who speaks lies or falsehood will within my presence stand.

Every morning I will go forth to find wicked in the land;
I will silence them forever, they won’t be allowed to stand.
I will cut off evildoers from the city of the Lord;
No iniquity must dwell there, where God’s presence is adored.

My heart, O God, is steadfast, before You, God and King;
To You I will make music; with all my soul, I’ll sing.
Awake, O harp and ly-re! I will awake the dawn.
I’ll praise You, Lord, ’midst nations; to peoples sing Your song.

Your love is great, and higher than are the heavens high;
Your faithfulness and truth, they do reach unto the sky.
Exalted o’er the heavens, O God, shall be Your worth;
And let Your wondrous glory be over all the earth.

Save us, O God, and help us with Your hand from above;
That we may be delivered—all those whom You do love.
God, from His sanctuary, said: “I will parcel out
in triumph, Succoth’s valley and Shechem ’round about.

“For Gilead is Mine, and Mine is Manasseh, too;
I’ll make Moab My washbowl; on Edom toss My shoe.
Eph-raim will be My helmet, My scepter Judah touts;
And over strong Phi-lis-tia, I will in triumph shout.”

Who will lead me to Edom, the city fortified?
Won’t You, O God, who’ve left us, who’ve not stayed on our side?
Give us Your aid against foes, for help of man is vain;
With God we will defeat foes; the vic-tor-y we’ll gain.

O thank the Lord, for He is good—His love endures always.
Let those of Is-ra-el now say, “His love endures always.”
And let the house of Aaron say, “His love endures always.”
Let those who fear the Lord now say, “His love endures always.”

In my distress, I called the Lord, and He did answer me;
I cried in anguish, and He heard—from trouble set me free.
The Lord is with me, ever sure; I will not be afraid.
For what is there that man can do, when I am on Him stayed?

The Lord’s with me; He is my help against the enemy;
Therefore, upon all of my foes I’ll look triumphantly.
It’s better far to trust the Lord than trust in man’s defense;
To find your refuge in the Lord than trust in king or prince.

The nations have surrounded me; they compass me about;
But in the Lord’s most holy name, I shall them all root out.
They came around on every side; they compassed me about;
But in the Lord’s most holy name, I shall them all root out.

They swarmed around me like fierce bees, like dry thorns set aflame;
They quickly died out, for I cut them off in the Lord’s name.
They pushed me back so that I fell, but then the Lord helped me.
The Lord is both my strength and song; and my salvation He.

In dwellings of the righteous ones is heard the joyous shout:
The Lord’s right hand does mighty things, salvation brings about.
The right hand of the mighty Lord is lifted up on high;
The Lord’s right hand does mighty things, and so I shall not die.

I shall not die, but live and tell His works with every breath;
The Lord has chastened me, but has not giv’n me o’er to death.
Now open unto me the gates, the gates of righteousness;
Then I will enter and give thanks, and ever the Lord bless.

This is the gate of the Lord God; the just shall enter there.
I will give thanks—You answered me and my salvation prayer.
That stone is made head cornerstone which builders did despise;
This is the doing of the Lord, and wondrous in our eyes.

This is the day the Lord has made; rejoice exceedingly.
O save us, Lord; grant us success and send prosperity.
For bless-ed is the one who comes in the name of the Lord;
From the Lord’s house, we bless you now—where blessing is outpoured.

The Lord is God, and He has made His light on us to rise;
Bind to the altar’s horns with cords the festal sacrifice.
You are my God; I’ll give You thanks, exalt You all my days.
O thank the Lord, for He is good—His love endures always.

“If the Lord had not been with us,” O let Is-ra-el now say:
“If the Lord had not been with us, when we were amidst the fray;
When attackers in their anger would have put us in the grave;
Torrents would have there engulfed us, raging floods swept us away.”

Bless-ed be the Lord who has not let us be torn from His care.
Like a bird, we have escaped from out of the cruel fowler’s snare.
For the snare has now been broken; we’ve escaped as in new birth;
Our help’s ever in the Lord’s name, who made heaven and the earth.

By rivers of cruel Babylon, there we sat down and wept;
When we remembered Zion’s land—our home we’ll not forget.

Upon the trees we hung our harps, for they demanded song;
Our captors, with triumphant scorn, said, “Sing songs of Zion.”

How can we sing songs of the Lord while in a foreign land?
If I forget Jerusalem, let skill leave my right hand.

May my tongue cleave to my mouth’s roof if I do not recall;
If I don’t praise Jerusalem, my chief joy above all.

Remember, Lord, all Edom’s sons who razed Jerusalem;
Who said, “Tear down, tear down its walls unto its foundation.”

O daughter of doomed Babylon, your end will surely come;
How bless’d will be the one who pays to you as you have done.

How bless’d will be the one who thus does end your cru-el-ty;
Who takes your infants, dashing them upon the rocks justly.

All praise unto the Lord my Rock, who trains my hands for war;
My fingers are well trained because the battle does endure.
He is my steadfast love, my strength, my fortress, and stronghold,
My shield in whom I refuge take, subduing peoples bold.

Lord, what is man that You know him, or that You think of him;
Man is no more than passing breath, like a mere shadow dim.
Now rend Your heavens, Lord, come down—touch mountains so they smoke;
Send forth Your lightning, part the skies—Your foes with fear provoke.

Send out Your arrows; rout the foe; stretch forth Your hand on high;
Deliver me from foreigners, from waters rising high.
The foreigner with lying tongue, whose falsehoods You despise—
O, Lord deliver me from him, whose hand is full of lies.

I’ll sing a new song to You, God, upon the harp I’ll sing;
Who rescues David from the sword; gives vic-to-ry to kings.
Deliver me and rescue me, from foreign ones who lie,
Whose mouths are full of enmity, who do Your truth defy.

May our sons in their youthfulness be like plants fully grown;
Our daughters like the pillars that adorn the palace throne.
May all our barns be always full, providing food each day;
May all our sheep give birth to young, ten thousand with no prey.

May all our cattle labor well; may our walls not be breached.
Lead us not to captivity; may fear not fill our streets.
How bless’d are those of whom these things are true and come to be;
How bless’d the ones whose God is Lord for all eternity.

Praise the Lord, my soul, O praise Him; to the Lord your praises give;
I will praise Him my whole life; sing praise to God long as I live.
Do not put your trust in princes; in mere man who cannot save—
When they die, their plans are nothing; they descend into the grave.

Bless-ed is the one whose help is Jacob’s God—how bless’d is he!
Bless-ed is the one whose hope is in the Lord his God, for He
is the Maker of the heavens, Maker of the earth and sea;
He, the Lord, made all that fills them, and keeps faith eternally.

He upholds the cause of those who are oppressed—to them is kind;
He gives food unto the hungry; and gives sight unto the blind.
The Lord sets the pris’ners free and lifts up those who are bowed down;
The Lord loves the righteous ones, and does their lives with glory crown.

The Lord watches o’er the strangers; and sustains the fatherless;
Helps the widows, but He frustrates wicked ones who would oppress.
The Lord God will reign forever; your God, Zion, evermore;
He reigns for all generations; hallelujah! Praise the Lord!

Praise the Lord! Praise from the heavens; praise Him in the heights above!
Praise Him, all His angels; praise Him, all His hosts who know His love.
Praise Him, sun and moon, and praise Him all you shining stars of light.
Praise Him, all you highest heavens, and you waters in the height.

Let them praise the Lord’s name, for He spoke and they all came to be;
He set them in place forever—everlasting His decree.
Praise the Lord from all the earth, all ocean depths and creatures there;
Lightning, hail, snow, clouds, and winds that do His bidding everywhere.

Praise Him, mountains and all hills, all fruit trees and all cedars too;
Creeping things and flying birds, all cattle and wild creatures too.
Kings of earth and all you nations, princes, rulers everywhere;
Young men, maidens, old men, children—let all earth His praise declare.

Let them ever praise the Lord’s name; His name is exalted high;
For His splendor and His glory are above the earth and sky.
He has lifted up a horn for His own people all their days;
Is-ra-el, the people near Him—give the Lord wholehearted praise!
